                <windows>

You are thinking about remodeling the left side (original section) of the house, and may choose to replace one or all of the existing 3 windows in that section. If you proceed to upgrade windows, I recommend you install ENERGY STARÂ® qualified, full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/mÂ²K. For your house, I modeled more efficient windows with a U-Value no higher than 1.05 W/mÂ²K.



Savings:

By replacing these 3 windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 61 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 975 ($325 per window) in rebates through the Greener Homes program.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</windows>
                <doors>

You are thinking about remodeling the left side (original section) of the house that may include the entrance located where the old &amp; new sections of housing adjoin. During this renovation, you may choose to upgrade the 2 existing doors (the main entrance and the garden doors) to new ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units. If you replace them, I recommend doors with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/mÂ²K or an Energy Rating of a minimum 34.



Savings:

By replacing these 2 entry door systems with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 15 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 0.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $325.00 in rebates (2 door units @ $162.50 each) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system (includes all windows in the door, sidelights and transom windows above) is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</doors>
                <VentilationSystem>

See the "Additional Comments" section of this report.</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

I have modeled a combination heating &amp; hot water system for this house - please see the recommendation in the Heating System section of this report.



</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is heated with a gas boiler and there is a separate 151 litre gas hot water tank to heat your domestic hot water (DHW). Both the boiler and the DHW have continuous pilot lights operating &amp; they are co-vented up a chimney. There is a 5" combustion air supply pipe located between the boiler &amp; DHW, however it was plugged with a towel when I was there. As discussed, the boiler &amp; hot water tank are located in the same room as thew clothes dryer &amp; the door to that room is normally shut. With a towel blocking the combustion air supply pipe, there is a high probability the clothes dryer could "backdraft" combustion fumes back down the chimney.



As per our discussion, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® 95% AFUE propane combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water. This system will direct vent to the outside and will no longer require the co-vented chimney pipe or the 6" combustion air supply pipe.



NOTE: You may want to work with the provider of your new boiler to design an in-floor radiant heat distribution system for the left side (original section) of your house, rather than just having the one exterior perimeter loop that currently exists. This may also provide an opportunity to better "air seal" this floor as there seems to be a lot of air leakage up &amp; through that section of floor.



Savings:

By upgrading to an ENERGY STARÂ® 95% AFUE gas fired combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water, you could potentially save $ 1,938 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 52.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,000 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an older gas fired boiler with an ENERGY STARÂ® certified gas fired boiler of the 95% efficiency that I modeled. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Renewable Energy Systems



The Arctic Energy Alliance administers the Alternative Energy Technologies Program (AETP) that includes rebates for Solar PV systems, including integrated battery energy storage for NWT residents who are not connected to the hydro-electric grid. 



Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the front facing roof (Southwest), directly connected to the Inuvik grid (ie: not including a battery storage system). This is estimated to produce about 3,188 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,000, and qualifying for up to $20,000 from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program.



For more information and pre-approval rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

https://aea.nt.ca/program/renewable-energy/



NOTE: The GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



Canada Greener Homes Grant 



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.

</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Pellet Stove Heat



You mentioned a possible interest in having a pellet stove installed in the main living area of the home (left side) during your renovations. As a first step, I recommend you upgrade the boiler &amp; hot water system of your home (see the Heating System section of this report).



Using our current Inuvik fuel cost library data for bagged wood pellets ($14 per bag - see the Fuel Cost data 4 sections below), I ran a quick calculation to see how much you would save (compared to the new combi boiler upgrade) if you used your wood pellet stove for 50% of the annual heating of the left side of your home.



The new combi boiler would be 95% efficient and I estimated the pellet stove at 82% efficiency and also being direct-vented (ie: having its' own dedicated outside combustion air supply directly connected/sealed to the back of the pellet stove). Your annual net heating cost is estimated to increase by $210 per year, so you may only want to consider a pellet stove as a backup or supplementary heating system and depend primarily on the new high efficiency combi boiler.



A qualified wood pellet stove is eligible for 50% of purchase cost up to $ 1,000 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Inuvik, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (for your house, just the clothes dryer, as there is no range hood or any bathroom exhaust fans), indicated a pressure drop of 1.8 Pascals. Although this number is currently lower than 5 Pascals I expect it to exceed 5 Pascals inside the mechanical room where the clothes dryer is located together with the boiler and hot water tank (and the door to that room is normally closed). Exceeding 5 Pascals indicates the use of this equipment may draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current boiler and hot water heater are both chimney vented and are susceptible to combustion back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke detectors but did not see any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - Kitchen range hood (not one currently installed &amp; venting to the outside)

        - 2 bathrooms, but no exhaust fans

        - The clothes dryer

        - A passive "make-up" air system with 2 x high wall vents at the end of the hall outside the bedrooms



If you choose to install a kitchen range hood and bathroom fans, please select ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units. For the new bathroom fans, you should consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



During the renovation of the left side (original section) of your home, you may want to consider installing a passive "make-up" fresh air system. These usually consist of one or two small pipes that come up from the unheated crawlspace below and through an interior wall up near the ceiling - usually located in a central hallway near bedrooms and/or in a central living room area. Each time and exhaust appliance operates, the fresh air supply allows some "make-up" air into the house - sometimes homeowners will have an adjustable grate over the fresh air supply to manually adjust them into a more open or closed position.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Inuvik



The following Nov. 1, 2022 values, before GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.69 per kWh thereafter

Synthetic Natural Gas (Utilidor Piped) - $35.44/Gigajoule

Oil  - $2.25 per litre 

Propane - $1.60 per litre delivered

Wood - $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $14.00 per bag, $675.00 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Contracted Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: k.baigent@aea.nt.ca

